WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.901
Plan mode and the explore, plan, implement rhythm.

2
00:00:03.181 --> 00:00:06.403
Claude Code’s plan mode makes the separation explicit.

3
00:00:06.683 --> 00:00:10.736
You enter it with Shift+Tab, /plan, or --permission-mode plan.

4
00:00:11.016 --> 00:00:17.629
In it, Claude reads files and runs shell commands to explore, then writes a plan, but doesn’t edit your source.

5
00:00:17.909 --> 00:00:25.291
When the plan is ready, you choose: approve and continue in auto mode, approve and approve each edit manually, or keep planning.

6
00:00:25.571 --> 00:00:30.755
Ctrl+G opens the plan in your editor so you can change it before approving.

7
00:00:31.035 --> 00:00:36.944
There’s also an opusplan model alias that uses Opus while planning and Sonnet while executing.

8
00:00:37.224 --> 00:00:40.509
Read a plan the way you’d read a design doc from a new hire.

9
00:00:40.789 --> 00:00:42.347
Does it name the right files?

10
00:00:42.627 --> 00:00:47.640
Does it identify the risky part (a migration, a public API, concurrency)?

11
00:00:47.920 --> 00:00:50.267
Does it say how the change will be verified?

12
00:00:50.547 --> 00:00:52.787
Does it propose anything you didn’t ask for?

13
00:00:53.067 --> 00:01:02.475
The plan is also where you notice the agent misunderstood the problem, which is far cheaper to fix than a 400-line diff built on the misunderstanding.

14
00:01:02.755 --> 00:01:12.717
When you don’t know exactly what you want yet, the best-practices page suggests flipping the roles: ask Claude to interview you until it could write the spec, then write it to SPEC.

15
00:01:12.997 --> 00:01:13.808
md.

16
00:01:14.088 --> 00:01:16.520
It will ask about the parts you haven’t pinned down.

17
00:01:16.800 --> 00:01:26.699
Then start a fresh session to build from the spec, so the build begins with clean context and a written definition of done, and point a reviewer at the same spec afterwards.
